workedExamples.question_text:
A gardener has $64$ m of fencing for three sides of a rectangular garden. An existing wall forms the fourth side. The side parallel to the wall is $x$ m long; each perpendicular side is $y$ m long.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[thick] (-0.4,2) -- (4.4,2);
\draw (0,2) -- (0,0) -- (4,0) -- (4,2);
\path (2,2) node[above] {wall};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$x$ m};
\path (0,1) node[left] {$y$ m};
\path (4,1) node[right] {$y$ m};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the area $A$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A poster has $120$ cm$^{2}$ of printed material. Its margins are $2$ cm at each side and $3$ cm at the top and bottom. The printed width is $x$ cm and printed height is $y$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (4,5);
\draw (0.6,0.9) rectangle (3.4,4.1);
\path (2,0.9) node[above] {$x$ cm};
\path (3.4,2.5) node[left] {$y$ cm};
\path (0.3,2.5) node[rotate=90] {$2$ cm};
\path (3.7,2.5) node[rotate=90] {$2$ cm};
\path (2,0.45) node {$3$ cm};
\path (2,4.55) node {$3$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the total poster area $A$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

An open box is made from an $18$ cm by $12$ cm sheet by cutting a square of side $x$ cm from each corner and folding up the sides.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (4.5,3);
\draw[dashed] (0.6,0) -- (0.6,3);
\draw[dashed] (3.9,0) -- (3.9,3);
\draw[dashed] (0,0.6) -- (4.5,0.6);
\draw[dashed] (0,2.4) -- (4.5,2.4);
\path (2.25,0) node[below] {$18$ cm};
\path (4.5,1.5) node[right] {$12$ cm};
\path (0,2.7) node[left] {$x$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the volume $V$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain for a non-degenerate box.

A gutter is made from a $32$ cm wide metal strip. A strip of width $x$ cm along each edge is bent up at a right angle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,1.4) -- (0,0) -- (4,0) -- (4,1.4);
\draw (0,0.2) -- (0.2,0.2) -- (0.2,0);
\draw (3.8,0) -- (3.8,0.2) -- (4,0.2);
\path (0,0.7) node[left] {$x$ cm};
\path (4,0.7) node[right] {$x$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the cross-sectional area $A$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A cylinder stands inside a cone of height $30$ cm and base radius $12$ cm. Its base lies in the cone's base plane and its top rim touches the cone. The cylinder has radius $r$ cm and height $h$ cm.
The diagram shows an axial cross-section.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,0) -- (0,5) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw (-1.2,0) -- (-1.2,2) -- (1.2,2) -- (1.2,0);
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (0,5);
\draw (0,2) -- (1.2,2);
\draw (0,2.2) -- (0.2,2.2) -- (0.2,2);
\path (0.6,2) node[above] {$r$ cm};
\path (1.2,1) node[left] {$h$ cm};
\draw[<->] (-2.6,0) -- (-2.6,5);
\path (-2.6,2.5) node[left] {$30$ cm};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6) -- (2,-0.6);
\path (1,-0.6) node[below] {$12$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use corresponding-side ratios to write the cylinder's volume $V$ as a function of $r$, and state the domain.

A rectangle is inscribed in a semicircle of radius $8$ cm, with its base on the diameter and centred at the circle's centre. Its width is $2x$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0);
\draw (2.5,0) arc (0:180:2.5);
\draw (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,2) -- (1.5,2) -- (1.5,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (1pt);
\draw (0,0) -- (1.5,2);
\path (0.75,1) node[above left] {$8$ cm};
\path (0,0) node[below=5mm] {$2x$ cm wide};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the rectangle's area $A$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A person rows across a river $800$ m wide, then walks along the opposite bank to a destination $6$ km downstream from the point directly opposite the start. Rowing speed is $3$ km/h and walking speed is $5$ km/h. The landing point is $x$ km downstream, where $0\leq x\leq6$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) -- (5,0);
\draw (0,2) -- (5,2);
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (0,2);
\draw (0,0) -- (2,2);
\fill (0,0) circle (1pt);
\fill (2,2) circle (1pt);
\fill (5,2) circle (1pt);
\path (0,0) node[below] {start};
\path (2,2) node[above] {landing};
\path (5,2) node[right] {destination};
\path (0,1) node[left] {$800$ m};
\path (1,2) node[below] {$x$ km};
\draw[<->] (0,2.8) -- (5,2.8);
\path (2.5,2.8) node[above] {$6$ km};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the total journey time $T$ in hours as a function of $x$.

foundation.question_text:
An open box is made from a $14$ cm by $9$ cm rectangle of card by cutting a square of side $x$ cm from each corner and folding up the sides.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (5.6,0) node[midway, below] {$14$ cm} -- (5.6,3.6) node[midway, right] {$9$ cm} -- (0,3.6) -- cycle;
\draw (0.8,0) -- (0.8,0.8) -- (0,0.8);
\draw (4.8,0) -- (4.8,0.8) -- (5.6,0.8);
\draw (0,2.8) -- (0.8,2.8) -- (0.8,3.6);
\draw (5.6,2.8) -- (4.8,2.8) -- (4.8,3.6);
\draw (0,2.8) -- (0,3.6) node[midway, left] {$x$ cm};
\draw[dashed] (0.8,0.8) -- (4.8,0.8) -- (4.8,2.8) -- (0.8,2.8) -- cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the volume $V$ cm$^{3}$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A closed cylindrical drum has a volume of $750$ cm$^{3}$, base radius $r$ cm and height $h$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,3) ellipse (1.2 and 0.45);
\draw (-1.2,0) arc (180:360:1.2 and 0.45);
\draw[dashed] (-1.2,0) arc (180:0:1.2 and 0.45);
\draw (-1.2,3) -- (-1.2,0);
\draw (1.2,3) -- (1.2,0) node[midway, right] {$h$ cm};
\draw (0,3) -- (1.2,3) node[midway, above] {$r$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the total surface area $S$ cm$^{2}$ as a function of $r$, and state the domain.

development.question_text:
A farmer has $90$ m of fencing for three sides of a rectangular paddock; a straight wall forms the fourth side.
The side parallel to the wall has length $x$ m and each of the other two sides has length $w$ m. [tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{
  tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings,
    mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}},
}
\draw[thick] (-0.6,2.6) -- (5.6,2.6);
\node[above] at (2.5,2.6) {wall};
\draw[tickmark] (0,2.6) -- (0,0) node[midway, left] {$w$ m};
\draw (0,0) -- (5,0) node[midway, below] {$x$ m};
\draw[tickmark] (5,0) -- (5,2.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the area $A$ m$^{2}$ as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A rectangular enclosure of area $600$ m$^{2}$ is divided into three equal pens by two internal fences, each parallel to the ends.
Each end has length $x$ m. [tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,2.5) -- (0,2.5) -- cycle;
\draw (2,0) -- (2,2.5);
\draw (4,0) -- (4,2.5);
\draw (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[midway, left] {$x$ m};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the total length $F$ m of fencing as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A rectangle has one side of length $x$ cm along the base of a right-angled triangle of base $18$ cm and height $12$ cm, with its opposite corner on the hypotenuse.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5.4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0,3.6);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$18$ cm};
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (A) node[midway, left] {$12$ cm};
\coordinate (a) at ($(A)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (b) at ($(A)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (a) -- ($(a)+(b)-(A)$) -- (b);
\draw (0,2.0) -- (2.4,2.0) node[midway, above] {$x$ cm};
\draw (2.4,2.0) -- (2.4,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the area $A$ cm$^{2}$ of the rectangle as a function of $x$, and state the domain.

A cylinder of radius $r$ cm and height $h$ cm stands inside a cone of base radius $9$ cm and height $24$ cm, with its base on the base of the cone and its top rim touching the curved surface.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
% View from above and in front: cone silhouettes and front base rim are solid;
% rear cone base rim and the entire enclosed cylinder base are hidden.
% Cone generators meet the base ellipse at its actual tangent points.
\pgfmathsetmacro{\coneAngle}{asin(0.5/6)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\coneTangentX}{2.25*sqrt(1-(0.5/6)^2)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\coneTangentY}{0.25/6}
\coordinate (coneLeft) at (-\coneTangentX,\coneTangentY);
\coordinate (coneRight) at (\coneTangentX,\coneTangentY);
\draw (coneLeft) arc[start angle={180-\coneAngle},end angle={360+\coneAngle},x radius=2.25,y radius=0.5];
\draw[dashed] (coneRight) arc[start angle=\coneAngle,end angle={180-\coneAngle},x radius=2.25,y radius=0.5];
\draw (coneLeft) -- (0,6) -- (coneRight);
% Solid internal top rim and vertical generators are instructional construction
% overlays showing the enclosed cylinder, not visible edges of the outer cone.
% Both cylinder rims use the same parameterised elliptical arc template.
\draw (-1.2,2.8) arc (180:360:1.2 and 0.28);
\draw (-1.2,2.8) arc (180:0:1.2 and 0.28);
\draw[dashed] (-1.2,0) arc (180:360:1.2 and 0.28);
\draw[dashed] (-1.2,0) arc (180:0:1.2 and 0.28);
\draw (-1.2,0) -- (-1.2,2.8);
\draw (1.2,0) -- (1.2,2.8);
\node at (0.6,1.4) {$h$ cm};
% Radius is an instructional measurement, not a surface edge.
\draw (0,2.8) -- (1.2,2.8) node[midway, below=4mm] {$r$ cm};
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (0,-1.3);
\draw[dashed] (2.25,0) -- (2.25,-1.3);
\draw[<->] (0,-1.15) -- (2.25,-1.15) node[midway, below] {$9$ cm};
\draw[dashed] (0,6) -- (-3.2,6);
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (-3.2,0);
\draw[<->] (-3.05,0) -- (-3.05,6) node[midway, left] {$24$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write the volume $V$ cm$^{3}$ of the cylinder as a function of $r$, and state the domain.

mastery.question_text:
A rectangle is inscribed in a semicircle of radius $6$ cm with one side along the diameter.
The rectangle has width $2x$ cm.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw (3,0) arc (0:180:3);
\draw (-1.9,0) -- (-1.9,2.32) -- (1.9,2.32) -- (1.9,0);
\draw (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[midway, below] {$2x$ cm};
\fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[dashed] (-3,0) -- (-3,-0.9);
\draw[dashed] (0,-0.45) -- (0,-0.9);
\draw[<->] (-3,-0.8) -- (0,-0.8) node[midway, below] {$6$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Write the area $A$ cm$^{2}$ of the rectangle as a function of $x$, and state the domain.
